//# #2745: an IMMEDIATELY INVOKED generic lambda.
//
// Codex, P1 round 19 on #2761 -- with a caveat worth recording, because it is
// the second time in nineteen rounds a report did not reproduce as written and
// the first time the finding was real anyway.
//
// The reported spelling, `((u: T) -> T { u })(a)`, was ALREADY refused: its
// parameter is annotated with the erased formal, and the guard for that fires
// first. Its concrete twin renders, so that refusal discriminates and is not a
// false one. But the DIAGNOSIS was right -- the callee is an `EFn`, the
// named-callee arm does not apply, and `infer_arg_type_name` only infers calls
// whose callee is an `EIdent`. The spelling that reaches it is the inline
// GENERIC one below: nothing in that lambda mentions `T`, so no other guard
// catches it. Measured on `c0e164c`: a 3-digit pointer decimal.
//
// Nothing needs recording for this case. The lambda is right there, so its
// return is read off it and applied through `dtd_interp_lamret_apply` -- the
// same rule a recorded one goes through, which is why the named and inline
// callees cannot answer differently.

struct Qt {
  w: Int
}

fn outer[T](x: T) -> String {
  let inner = [T](a: T) -> String {
    "\{([U](u: U) -> U { u })(a)}"
  }
  inner(Qt::{ w: 5 })
}

export fn _start() -> Int {
  String::length(outer(7))
}
